.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH qsh-identity 1  "identity 0.7.2" 
.SH NAME
qsh\-identity \- Manage this device\*(Aqs own identity (`docs/CLI.md` §6.11)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBqsh identity\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR] [\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-punch\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-iroh\fR] [\fB\-\-rendezvous\fR] [\fB\-\-jump\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Manage this device\*(Aqs own identity (`docs/CLI.md` §6.11)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single `qsh.cli/v1` JSON envelope on stdout instead of human\-readable text
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Emit newline\-delimited JSON on stdout (reserved for streaming commands; non\-streaming commands emit a single line, same as `\-\-json`)
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic verbosity on stderr (`\-v` info, `\-vv` debug, `\-vvv` trace). Never affects stdout (`docs/CLI.md` §2.2)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress diagnostic output on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR
Never run ssh on this command\*(Aqs behalf: no automatic bootstrap of an unknown name, and no restarting of a pinned host\*(Aqs serve over ssh when the connection fails (`docs/CLI.md` §7). `qsh pair ssh` is not affected; it is ssh by definition
.TP
\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR
When the ssh bootstrap finds no `qsh` on the remote host (or one too old to answer it), install this machine\*(Aqs release into `~/.local/bin` there without asking (`docs/CLI.md` §7). Works with `\-\-json`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR
Never install or upgrade `qsh` on the remote host, and do not ask; a missing `qsh` ends as `remote_qsh_missing`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-punch\fR
Never start a hole punch on this command\*(Aqs behalf, and send no `PunchRequest` (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0054 decision 19). The route is the direct dial alone. Wins over `[punch].mode`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-iroh\fR
Never take the iroh relay route on this command, whatever the host\*(Aqs pin `routes` and `[iroh].mode` say, and contact no relay (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0061 decision 1). On `qsh serve` and `qsh listen` it also keeps the iroh endpoint down, which they otherwise bring up unless `[iroh].enable = false` (ADR\-0063). Not stored
.TP
\fB\-\-rendezvous\fR \fI<PEER>\fR
Use this pinned peer as the rendezvous for hole punching on this command, instead of the host\*(Aqs own `rendezvous` hints (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0054 decision 4). Must be pinned with an address. Not stored
.TP
\fB\-\-jump\fR \fI<HOPS>\fR
Reach the host through these pinned peers, first hop first, for this command: `a` or `a,b` (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0056 decision 20). Each hop is the name of a pin; a `user@` or `:port` on it is ignored by the QUIC path. `none` goes the ordinary way even when the host\*(Aqs pin has a `jump`. Wins over `\-o ProxyJump` and over the pin\*(Aqs own `jump`. Not stored. The short `\-J` is the same flag on the commands that take it
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
qsh\-identity\-export(1)
Print this device\*(Aqs certificate as PEM, never its private key (`docs/CLI.md` §6.11, ADR\-0013). Without `\-\-out`, the PEM is the only thing this command writes to stdout, so it pipes directly into `qsh trust add \-\-cert\-file \-` on another host
.TP
qsh\-identity\-move\-key(1)
Move this device\*(Aqs private key to another key store (`file` or `platform`) without changing the device id, certificate or fingerprint, so peers\*(Aq pins and ACL rows stay valid (`docs/CLI.md` §6.11, ADR\-0032). A local command: it cannot be run against a remote host. Run it before an always\-on service (a LaunchDaemon, systemd linger) if the OS credential store is not reachable from there, as `qsh doctor` reports
.SH VERSION
v0.7.2
